NAME
       sudo - run a command as root

SYNOPSIS
       **sudo** __command__ [__arg__...]
       **sudo -i**
       **sudo -s**
       **sudo -h** | **--help**

DESCRIPTION
       **sudo** lets an administrator run a single command, or a whole
       shell, with root privileges. It asks for the password of the user
       who runs it, not the password of root, so root does not need a
       password of its own for administrators to work.

       Only members of the **wheel** group may use sudo. The installer puts
       a user into wheel when the question "Should NAME be a superuser
       (sudo, group wheel)?" is answered with yes. Other users are refused
       right away, before their password is checked.

       When sudo is run by root it does not ask for a password and runs
       __command__ directly.

HOW IT WORKS
       sudo itself has no special rights: it is an ordinary program, not a
       setuid binary. The check is done by the kernel:

       1.  sudo reads the password from the terminal with echo turned off.

       2.  It passes the password to the kernel with the **SYS_SUDO**
           system call (number 397, **pc_sudo()** in libc).

       3.  The kernel looks up the calling user in /etc/passwd, checks
           that the user is in the wheel group of /etc/group and verifies
           the password against the hash in /etc/shadow.

       4.  On success the kernel changes the user and group of the calling
           process to 0 (root). The password buffer is wiped both in sudo
           and in the kernel.

       5.  sudo then sets a few environment variables and replaces itself
           with __command__ (execvp) or with /bin/sh.

       Because the process itself becomes root, everything it starts
       afterwards runs as root too, until it exits. The rights are never
       given to the parent shell.

OPTIONS
       **-i**
           Start an interactive root shell (/bin/sh) in /root, with
           **HOME** set to /root. Leave it with **exit**.

       **-s**
           Start an interactive root shell in the current directory.
           **HOME** is set to /root as well.

       **-h**, **--help**
           Print a short usage line and exit.

       The first argument that is not one of these options starts
       __command__; every argument after it is passed to the command
       unchanged, so options of the command itself need no **--**.

THE WHEEL GROUP
       A user is a member of wheel when either is true in /etc/group:

       -   the user's primary group (the fourth field of /etc/passwd) is
           the group id of wheel, or

       -   the user name is listed in the member field of the wheel line.

       The wheel line has the usual format __name__:__password__:__gid__:__members__,
       with members separated by commas:

           wheel:x:10:alice,bob

       To make a user an administrator, add the name to that list as
       root, for example with **sudo vim /etc/group**. To take the rights
       away, remove the name again. The change takes effect for the next
       sudo call; no restart or new login is needed.

PASSWORDS
       sudo asks for the password every time it runs. There is no grace
       period during which a second sudo call goes through without a
       password, and no ticket or timestamp file is kept.

       While typing, Backspace deletes the last character. Enter ends the
       input. Typing is not shown on the screen. After three wrong
       passwords sudo gives up.

       The password hashes in /etc/shadow are written by the installer
       and checked by the kernel's own password hashing (purecrypt).

NETWORK WITHOUT SUDO
       Members of wheel do not need sudo to manage Wi-Fi: scanning,
       connecting and disconnecting are also allowed for them, for the
       user of the current graphical session and for root. This is what
       lets the network menu of the desktop work for an administrator.

ENVIRONMENT
       sudo keeps the environment of the user and only changes these
       variables:

       **SUDO_USER**
           The name of the user who ran sudo.

       **USER**, **LOGNAME**
           Set to root.

       **HOME**
           Set to /root, but only with **-i** and **-s**. A plain
           **sudo** __command__ keeps the user's HOME, so files a program
           writes to ~ may end up in the user's home directory, owned by
           root.

       **PATH** is not changed: __command__ is looked up in the PATH of the
       user who runs sudo.

FILES
       /etc/passwd
           User names, user ids and primary groups.

       /etc/group
           The wheel group and its members.

       /etc/shadow
           Password hashes. Only root can read it.

       /bin/sh
           The shell started by **-i** and **-s**.

DIAGNOSTICS
       **Sorry, try again.**
           The password was wrong. sudo asks again.

       **sudo: 3 incorrect password attempts**
           Three wrong passwords; the command is not run.

       **NAME is not in the wheel group. This incident has been logged.**
           The user is not an administrator. The password is not checked.

       **sudo: COMMAND: No such file or directory**
           __command__ was not found in PATH.

       The kernel writes every attempt to the kernel log. A successful one
       looks like

           sudo: alice (uid=1000) became root, pid=42

       and a refused one like

           sudo: denied for bob (uid=1001 pid=43), not in wheel

       Read them with **journalctl**.

EXIT STATUS
       The exit status of __command__ or of the root shell. Otherwise:

       **0**    **-h** was given.
       **1**    Wrong password, not in wheel, input ended (Ctrl-D) before
              Enter, or no command was given.
       **126**  __command__ was found but cannot be run.
       **127**  __command__ was not found, or /bin/sh could not be started.

DIFFERENCES FROM OTHER SUDO
       This sudo is much smaller than the sudo of other systems:

       -   There is no /etc/sudoers. The only rule is membership in wheel,
           and a member may run any command.

       -   A command always runs as root. There is no **-u** to run it as
           another user and no **-g** for another group.

       -   There is no **-l**, **-v**, **-k** or **-e** (sudoedit). Edit
           protected files with **sudo vim** __file__.

       -   The password is asked every time; see PASSWORDS.

       -   The environment is kept as it is; see ENVIRONMENT.

SECURITY
       A root shell started with **-i** or **-s** stays root until it is
       closed. Close it when the work is done.

       Because PATH is kept, a program with the same name earlier in the
       user's PATH is run instead of the system one. Use a full path,
       such as **sudo /bin/rm**, when in doubt.

EXAMPLES
       Remove a file that belongs to root:

           sudo rm /etc/motd

       Edit a system file:

           sudo vim /etc/hostname

       Make the user alice an administrator:

           sudo vim /etc/group
           # change the wheel line to: wheel:x:10:alice

       Install and update packages:

           sudo pkg -Syu

       Open a root shell in /root and leave it again:

           sudo -i
           exit

       Check which user a command runs as:

           sudo id

